<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Kandahar</h1><p class="st-meta">1818 CE – 1855 CE (approx.) · capital Kandahar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The principality ruled by the Barakzai 'Kandahar Sardars', half-brothers of Dost Mohammad Khan, between the fall of the Durranis and Afghan reunification.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>As the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire</a> fell apart after 1818, the powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barakzai-dynasty">Barakzai</a> brothers divided its lands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> went to a group of brothers known as the Kandahar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardar">Sardars</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purdil-khan">Purdil Khan</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kohandil-khan">Kohandil Khan</a>, who were half-brothers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dost-mohammad-khan">Dost Mohammad Khan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardar">sardars</a> ruled jointly over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashtuns">Pashtun</a> tribes, relying on tribal levies and revenue from trade on the route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. They negotiated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> as well as with their relatives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-afghan-war">First Anglo-Afghan War</a> the British occupied Kandahar from 1839 to 1842, and the sardars took refuge in Persia before returning.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kohandil-khan">Kohandil Khan</a> died in 1855, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dost-mohammad-khan">Dost Mohammad Khan</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> and incorporated it into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-afghanistan">Emirate of Afghanistan</a>, a key step towards reuniting the Afghan lands.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-kandahar.jpg" alt="Principality of Kandahar" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: AfghanTsakhtan · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Kandahar_(1818).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1818 CE – 1855 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kandahar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Purdil Khan, Kohandil Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Pashto, Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kandahar Sardars, Qandahar Sardars</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1836&amp;focus=principality-of-kandahar" aria-label="Principality of Kandahar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6375 1026 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Kandahar lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6695 1282L6757 1183M6695 1282L6757 1183"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6757 1183h0M6757 1183h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6695 1282h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6695 1282h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kandahar. <a href="/atlas?year=1836&amp;focus=principality-of-kandahar">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
